we're in seven different states  uh some in the northeast  mid-atlantic and and down senator  wicker's uh nick of the woods down in uh  mississippi  um the for those  for those properties most of our  business comes from  an hour's drive and we were a bit more  insulated um barring the closure  obviously but we were a bit more  insulated  um in las vegas because the barriers of  travel  were just easier you can jump in your  car and not have to deal with airfare  and that sort of thing um las vegas  um and and i'll parallel that too with  we do have two  semi-destination resorts one of which is  in biloxi mississippi  um that property also was hampered more  as compared to the other seven as an  example it behaved a little bit more  like las vegas  and it also brought out in atlantic city  had similar um characteristics to  recovery  in las vegas but nowhere near as  devastating um  it's your point in and you've heard the  testimony from from the panelists  um the getting getting customers  comfortable with air travel primarily  is paramount and um and it's to take  some time to get international travel  to come back in earnest particularly in  las vegas which is vitally important  we know those customers stay longer um  they enjoy our restaurants they enjoy  our casinos  they enjoy our shows they've spent quite  a bit on retail  and they're vitally important to the  embattled and las vegas economy so  um anything that we can do certainly to  reduce those barriers would be  incredibly helpful especially for las  vegas  thank you mr hill i'd like to ask you a  similar question  but really about our conventions we know  that conventions  business travel is the lifeblood of not  just  las vegas but so many other cities  across the country  so uh how do you feel about uh what can  we do to bring that ecotourism back what  do we need  and its importance to our communities  well thank you chair rosen uh i  iranian remember scott earlier mentioned  the  connection between 