 you and now recognize ranking  member scott  thank you chair rosen i want to thank  each of the witnesses for your testimony  um ms dover in your written testimony  you state that the travel and tourism  industry is facing historic labor  shortages  which could significantly restrict the  industry from recovering from this  severe downturn  can you share the reasons behind this  would you be able to  give you some examples from your members  as well as what they've done to mitigate  this issue uh and just go ahead and talk  about some of the challenges your  members are facing  with regard to labor shortages  yes thank you very much senator scott so  as i stated in my other statement that  we're  in competition with our unemployment  system  so many people are making more money  staying home  and we're having a really tough time  getting people to even want to apply for  jobs  we've been hosting job fairs some of our  members are even offering bonuses  and people are still not showing up to  apply for the jobs  just a couple of examples senator that  we're hearing from our members i have a  member yesterday who's telling me that  they haven't had their garbage picked up  in  almost a week and and so this is bigger  than just  the hotel and restaurant industry the  waste management industry they don't  have drivers  we got notice from our food distribute  uh distribution companies and  our beverage companies that they may not  be receiving their products in a timely  manner because  they too don't have enough drivers  applying for jobs so  one of the things that we are in hopes  is that we can  find between both state and federal  clearly unemployment is important for  many people but there are so many jobs  available right now  that if we could go back to a system  where you used to have to show  that you had applied and been turned  down for several jobs three jobs i  believe is what it was in florida  that um if we could reverse some of  those  mandates then i believe that we could  begin to see people  want to go back to work so we are  desperate  and anything that you all can do in  congress to help us  relieve this burden i hear from my  colleagues all across the country that  this is not just a florida issue that  it's everywhere  so thank you for for asking and allowing  me the opportunity to talk to you about  our  very uh critical labor shortage so  missed over so if somebody  if an employer tells an employee that  they have a job openings in my  understanding nationwide we have 7.4  million job openings  if an employer says they have a job  opening does the employee  be is are they able to continue on  unemployment or do they  are they required to come back to work  well they're not required senator my  understanding to come back to work  they can make the decision and what  we're trying to  also do education to many of the people  who are not coming back to work is to  remind  them that these jobs will not be  available forever  i mean we've all you've heard us all  today on the panel talk about the need  to rebound and we will rebound this is  we're a resilient country and we will  rebound  but one day these jobs that people are  turning down  won't be available anymore so we're  reminding people to  take these jobs while they're available  unemployment in florida is already down  to  a little over four percent so it's not  long before  you know we're gonna be in a very low  unemployment  and nearly an unemployable so hopefully  we can get people to  see that we have great jobs available  florida is the tourism mecca we and we  have  just hopefully people will see the  importance of coming back to work  thank you mr orton um ms barnes we've  seen the cruise industry as stand still  under the current cdc restrictions with  no timeline in place for return what  impact  has this had not only on the cruise  industry but throughout the travel and  tourism industries  thank you for the question um you know  we very much  are supportive of the legislation that  you and senator uh sullivan introduced  today along with senator rubio  um because we really do think that the  entire industry needs to have uh clear  guidelines on how to reopen  uh with uh of the timeline and a date  certain uh to do so  uh and and really we are normally uh  used to welcoming over  13 million uh travelers via cruise ship  annually uh to the united states and the  impact that that has on  ports and destinations throughout the  country uh restaurants  attractions uh folks that are uh being  able to  to sell um uh  gifts for their families uh at those  ports and in those cities  uh so the impact really has been quite  significant  and we really believe that every segment  of the industry  should be able again to reopen uh and  that it isn't fair uh not to have  clear guidelines so uh we hope that the  the  cdc uh will be able to put forward uh  clear guidelines in the in the immediate  term because the economic consequences  really are significant not only to  florida but to to the  other countries like alaska louisiana  california washington and other states  across the country thank you thank you  joe rosen  thank you senator scott i'd like to next  recognize via webex senator  klobuchar thank you very much  uh chair rosen and uh thank you as well  to  uh senator scott um and i'm really  excited to hear from our witnesses today  i know this has been such a tough time  for tourism but  having this hearing couldn't be more  timely um  i have long been involved in this issue  from the time that i had  the job of chairing the tourism  subcommittee in commerce  um proud of the work that we've done  together with some of the witnesses on  brand usa  and the work that you're doing today and  i guess i'd start there because i know  that  um senator rosen asked a question and  talked about the loss at least of  international travel  on the mgm resorts and also really  everywhere in the country which is part  of the reason we were so proud of the  work we've done  on brand usa which finally allowed us to  have an even playing field when it came  to  promoting our own country this has been  a real gut punch for the tourism  industry  obviously uh we had to shut down the  borders for  international tourists but as we see  this as we call it in  on lake superior the lighthouse on the  horizon as opposed to light at the end  of the tunnel  um with the vaccine and what's happening  um  ms barnes in your testimony you  highlight that if international travel  is not reopened soon a total of 1.1  million american jobs  and 262 billion in spending will be lost  by the end of 2021  and that temporary emergency funding for  brand usa is needed uh can you talk  about what resources you think  are needed sure thank you senator for  your um  question and for your tremendous  leadership as the chair of the  travel and tourism caucus in the senate  uh we are very grateful for your  leadership uh  in particular as we sought to  reauthorize brand usa  uh back in late 2019 which feels like a  decade ago  except you must admit we were smart to  do it early  we got it done a year early thanks to  senator blunt and other events  yes great both of you thank you um so as  i noted  uh international travels declined by 76  percent  uh and right now we're looking at a  five-year time horizon for recovery  but we know that we can shorten that  timeline uh and  just in in the losses for 2021  uh if we are able to reopen  international travel by july  um that can stimulate 40 of the the  expected  expected losses this year so we really  need brand usa  uh that is currently uh having a funding  challenge because of  um the lack of international travel  usually the esta fe  uh is coming in in a robust fashion and  capped at 100 million which goes to  brand usa but  because that money isn't coming in  because the international borders are  closed  we really need an emergency funding  mechanism uh what we think is about 250  million dollars  in an appropriation usually the 100  million that comes in on international  fees is matched by the private sector  but unfortunately due to the decimation  of the private sector right now  those matching funds are are unlikely to  be captured so  again if if the appropriations uh  committee could could put forward 250  million  we think they'll be able to do their  work to bring back international  travelers  and make sure that that 26 to 1 return  on investment is uh  is provided and i'll just note since we  were talking about exports that  in 2019 the tourism industry provided a  59 billion dollar  export for the for the country so it  really is  uh important beyond uh just domestic  funding  very good and then another bill uh that  senator blunt and i have  introduced the protecting tourism in the  united states act  directs the commerce department uh in  consultation with the u.s  travel and tourism board and key federal  agencies to develop a plan  uh to help the tourism industry recover  simply because  you know i think that as things are  getting a little better where this their  their ramp up is going to be slower by  virtue of the nature  of the customers here and where they  come from and how they can travel  than other industries and i guess i'll  turn to  you on to you with us mr perez and by  the way  um i'm also want to mention the great  leadership of  uh chair rosen as well as senator cortez  masto  on these issues um with nevada  but one report found that in 2020 more  than 670 000 hotel industry jobs  and in your testimony you know that mgm  resorts had to furlough  60 000 employees as a result of the  pandemic  can you speak to how long the hotel  industry can sustain itself on the  current federal  economic relief particularly regarding  your workforce thank you very much  senator for the question  um look the  employees are coming back uh not only  in las vegas but also for our regional  properties  but they're 